  this is a tributary that feeds the grand  rivers that sustain the amazon forest  just look at these pictures  and think about that  because they are not only destroying  trees here they're poisoning rivers too  for joe farah a local campaigning to  save the waterways of manaus it is  heartbreaking you'll not see it  it's just terrible i was born here i  used to swim in this river he told me  it's not only what you see here that's  so depressing it's what you smell it's  industrial waste chemicals and human  sewage flowing through here  this is what man is doing to one of the  greatest and most important rainforests  in the world  much of the pollution is from  uncontrolled housing settlements this  entire area was rainforest just 20 years  ago  most of the 148 small rivers that cross  the city are little more than channels  of human waste  they are dying  on their banks i found the desperately  poor trying to exist amid hopelessness  and disease  understandably for people like mother  and daughter rosa and gaisa it is daily  survival they care about not the fate of  the rainforest or fears of climate  change  all i know is every day here is worse  because the government won't help us  says rosa  marcus castro a professor of urban  geography here says it's too easy to  blame the poor problem  the problem here is there is such  inequality and no proper planning he  says so they have no choice  he thinks rather look to a government  that day by day is allowing development  to eat into the rainforest  river campaigner joe farah is not  prepared to let the rivers here die in  the forest on the edge of the city he  and his team are planting new trees on  the banks of a river he's determined to  preserve my first tree here  regeneration and regrowth that is badly  needed here  and below them pure water running as it  should do  and i've got to say when you travel  around manaus on the outskirts of manaus  it really is notable how much  unregulated and uncontrolled development  is taking place and the big question of  course is when will it all  stop  for now from the amazon that is about  all let's go back to anna in glasgow  mark thanks very much indeed  theresa may was prime minister when her  then foreign secretary boris johnson  seized on the chance for the uk to host  cop 26 a summit that was originally due  to take place in italy well mrs may is  here in glasgow this week and i spoke to  her today in her only television  interview here at cop  and asked whether she rates the chances  of success higher or lower than the 6  out of 10 that mr johnson has touted  i'm confident that there will be success  here at cop26 if you think back to paris  and the paris accord that actually came  together at the last moment but what  matters about cop 26 is it's not a sort  of end point uh what matters is we get  momentum for action coming out of it and  i'm sure that's what we're going to get  when you voted against the government's  foreign aid cards you said then that the  government had broken its promise to the  poorest people of the world  so do you think that developing nations  can trust the uk leadership on climate  action and could that ultimately be a  barrier to success at cop26 i think what  everybody's here about is what we're  going to do about climate change what  the uk has done has shown that important  leadership in being the first major  country to set that net zero target of  2050 and that has been leading the way  and now boris and the government have  published the net zero strategy showing  how we actually get there and that's  what we want coming out of cop26  and you can see more of my conversation  with theresa may after this program on  sky news tonight so do stay tuned for  that  now let's talk about what we've heard  today with rachel kite who is dean of  the fletcher school at tufts university  and former special representative of the  un secretary general for sustainable  energy for all and kave gillampur vice  president for international strategies  at the center for climate and energy  solutions welcome to both of you  so what a day the day one proper of  cop26 carvey boris johnson has talked  about the risk of serious failure at  this summit as a former negotiator both  of you former negotiators what's your  feeling are you feeling optimistic as  cop gets underway well i think the good  news is that yesterday when the former  negotiations started they got off to a  smooth start which is a very good sign  and actually quite unusual there's  usually procedural problems at the  beginning but all the formal agendas for  the meetings were adopted without  problems and i think that bodes well  that negotiators from around the world  have come to be constructive and try to  find a deal so i think the first hurdle  was passed with success yesterday that's  very interesting i know you've been in  meetings today rachel so what are you  picking up what's the mood that you're  sensing well we've seen a  lifting of ambition by india we saw a  very strong speech by the secretary  general saying that he wants everybody  to run within the guard rails and make  sure that all of these promises actually  come true i've been in meetings with the  private sector who are coming and  talking about mobilizing finance some of  the extraordinary innovation the  partnerships that they're forming so you  get this feeling that there's actually a  lot going on and of course you can feel  the pressure from the street and  actually the pressure from the river as  well so you sound quite positive do you  think that we will get to the end of  this and say that cop has been a success  well i think that it's the gap between  uh instrumenting policy if you're a  government and then what the private  sector civil society and everybody else  thinks we can do if we can close that  gap so that governments leave here  believing that they can do more that  they can go faster then today was a good  day in terms of setting that direction  well yes and and that's why this cop is  so interesting isn't it in paris there  was a very binary issue either you got a  deal or you didn't get a deal this time  it's all about how much momentum is  there to keep global warming capped at  1.5 it's going to be quite hard to  measure success isn't it i think so i  mean i think the important thing to  remember is that the paris agreement was  never set up to solve the problem in one  go and it has a cycle to raise ambition  over time and with each cop that  ambition is being raised as rachel said  we saw india step up today brazil the us  has set out its long-term strategy so  whereas in paris we were heading for  four degrees we're heading now for  2.7 or less so it seems to be working  and success may not be known for some  time after this i'm so interested that  both of you have said that it's good  news that india has set out its target  and yet there will be some that will say  but they've said they'll reach net zero  by 2070 which is 20 years after the  target date of this summit and 10 years  behind the likes of of china and russia  so why do you feel this is good news  well because i think that most countries  are going to  over deliver against their promises  because as technology improves as we  understand how better to do this as the  global economy moves so i think that the  hedging of the bets you see in these  kinds of promises might fade away but  also remember that the 2050 target the  1.5 is for the global economy and it was  always understood that some developing  countries would take longer than others  the big stumbling block potentially here  is finance  biden's got a bigger promise that he's  made he's got a plan but the us is far  behind on finance so tomorrow is going  to be a critical day when we see whether  or not there's any money on the table  well yes finance so important and i know  that you've represented the marshall  islands haven't you one of those nations  that risks going underwater because of  climate change  countries like that do they feel they  have a voice do they feel they're being  listened to when they say we still  haven't got our 100 billion dollars we  were promised back in 2009 it was due by  last year we're told we may not get it  for another three years how how how do  they feel could this be a stumbling  block it could be a stumbling block  and not only is there the issue of the  hundred billion on the table at this cop  we also have to decide what happens in  the long term after 2025  and it's not only about the hundred  billion it's really about shifting the  trillions is the phrase how do you align  the global economy  with the net zero target so how does the  financial sector businesses and the  whole global economy align so it's  actually much it's much bigger issue  than 100 billion but that's an important  symbol of trust well yes and so we're  going to listen out for for the issue of  climate finance through the week i mean  mark carney is is working behind the  scenes himself do you think we'll get  some good news on that or not what's  your instinct yeah so mark is talking  about 130 trillion dollars of money and  assets under management committed to net  zero i think that the civil society is  concerned that some of the fine print  there would allow banks that are still  financing coal to be in the club but i  think the point is get them in talking  and then work with them to again ratchet  up their ambition as they learn how to  do this but i think the difference  between now and paris was in paris we  thought that we had a little bit more  time the international energy agencies  report that came out two to three weeks  ago basically said there should be no  more finance going into any new oil gas  and coal and i think that's the cut off  so civil society will say we want to see  that cut off the finance sector will say  give us time and that's the that's the  process oh it's going to be fascinating  all those meetings down those corridors  come back and tell us how they're going  won't you but we must leave it now uh  rachel kite and carve input thanks very  much indeed thank you thank you  and don't forget that you can listen to  a new climate cast every day from cop 26  wherever you find your podcasts and uh  you may well already be watching this on  our pop-up channel but if you're not you  can find uninterrupted coverage from  cop26 that's between 9am and 7pm on  channel 525 all week
 

